From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: a733: KMS -- one CRTC, one plane, one connector, /dev/fb0

[Recipe cut: regenerated: the hunks that touched the removed DE/TCON debugfs
knobs are gone.]

The drm_device this pixel path has never had. Eleven of the fourteen patches
that built the path are HW-proven -- AUX and EDID (`drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux`, plus an earlier
EDID extension-block reader that this recipe does not carry), the main-link
PHY (`drm-a733-dp-configure-the-main-link-phy`), TCON_TV1's raster (`drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source`), the DisplayPort video stream (`drm-a733-dp-the-main-video-stream`),
link training (`drm-a733-dp-link-training`) and the display engine that pulls pixels out of DRAM
(`drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1`, first pixels on 2026-08-19) -- and none of it is reachable except by
hand, through debugfs, because no drm_device binds on this SoC. Nothing calls
drm_client_setup(), so there is no /dev/fb0 and there is no console.

This is the second bring-up stage: a component master
and a drm_driver, one CRTC on TCON_TV1, one plane out of one display-engine
channel, an encoder and drm_bridge_connector_init() so that `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`'s bridge has
something to hang on, and drm_client_setup() + drm_fbdev_dma.

WHAT IS PROVEN HERE, AND IT IS NOT MUCH
----------------------------------------

⬜ NOTHING IN THIS PATCH HAS RUN ON SILICON. It applies at --fuzz=0, it passes
compile-check, checkpatch --strict is clean on every file it adds or touches,
and the a733_defconfig round-trip is clean. That is the whole of the claim. The
blocks underneath are proven; the frame around them is not, and those are
different statements.

⬜ THE OBSERVABLE THAT WOULD CLOSE IT:
/dev/fb0 at 1920x1200; a known pattern written into it is on the screen; the
same pattern read back and compared with cmp matches. FALSIFIED BY: no
/dev/fb0; a cmp mismatch; or a screen that does not change when the buffer
does. A small test script is the
instrument and needs no new code.

⬜ AND ONE GATE IS UNMET BY CONSTRUCTION. `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1`'s register set is not currently
reproducible -- a test run gave a black screen with a bit-identical set and the
CCSC correction that answers it has not been on a board. So this patch can put
a /dev/fb0 in front of an engine that produces no picture, and the two failures
look nothing alike: no /dev/fb0 is this patch, a black /dev/fb0 that reads back
correctly is `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1`'s open CCSC question. Read
`drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1`'s header before attributing either.

THE ONE LOAD-BEARING LINE
--------------------------

  .fb_create = drm_gem_fb_create        NOT drm_gem_fb_create_with_dirty

drm_fbdev_dma_driver_fbdev_probe() branches on fb->funcs->dirty
(drm_fbdev_dma.c:315). With a .dirty callback the fbdev client allocates a
SHADOW buffer and defers copies into the real scanout buffer -- so /dev/fb0
would not be the memory the display engine fetches, reading it back would
compare a shadow with itself, and a poke into it would not reach the screen.
Both halves of the falsifier above, silently defeated. This hardware scans DRAM
continuously and needs no damage tracking.

⚠ THE VENDOR'S OWN DRIVER AGREES, AND THAT IS EVIDENCE OF A KIND CODE USUALLY
IS NOT: sunxi_drm_gem_fb_funcs (allwinner-bsp, drivers/drm/sunxi_drm_drv.c:
109-112) carries .destroy and .create_handle and no .dirty, and its fbdev maps
the client buffer directly (drm_client_buffer_vmap(),
sunxi_fbdev_platform.c:269). The driver putting a picture on the reference
board right now also scans out of the buffer userspace writes.

WHAT IT REUSES, WHICH IS MOST OF IT
------------------------------------

sun4i_drv.c is the model for the component master; the atomic helpers,
drm_gem_dma, drm_bridge_connector, drm_dp_helper and the fbdev client are used
as they are. New code is a plane, a CRTC and the wiring: about 600 lines
including a header comment that is a third of the file.

⚠ WHERE THIS DEVIATES FROM the full-reuse plan, under which
the display engine should JOIN sun4i-drm rather than be driven from here:

  1. an audit of that move found it
     costs FOUR changes to shared mainline code -- sun8i_mixer.h's map[6]->[7],
     a fourth de-top regmap in sun8i_mixer.c, a DE33 base in sun8i_csc.c that
     is a latent upstream defect nothing shipped has ever executed, and a new
     compatible in a closed DE2-mixer schema -- three of them
     mandatory-or-it-does-not-work.
  2. The move is gated on D0, `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` reproducing on a board, and D0 is
     not met (above). The condition for moving is a change of frame around a
     KNOWN-GOOD register set.
  3. Nothing here forecloses it. When the mixer entry lands, this file's plane
     is deleted and the CRTC calls sunxi_engine_layers_init() instead. The
     device tree does not move at all -- `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` already claims the DE33 mixer
     compatible for exactly that reason.

⚠ AND THE CRTC: that plan prefers refactoring mainline's sun4i_crtc.c onto an
ops struct over mirroring it. Mirrored here, for reasons that are structural
and not effort:

  - sun4i_crtc is coupled to TWO mainline types. It holds a struct sun4i_tcon *
    AND a struct sunxi_engine *, and its init calls sunxi_engine_layers_init()
    to create the planes (sun4i_crtc.c:194-208). Reusing it needs the TCON ops
    refactor AND our engine to BE a sunxi_engine -- i.e. deviation 1 above.
  - sun4i_crtc_init() carries no EXPORT_SYMBOL and sun4i_crtc.o links into
    sun4i-tcon.ko, not sun4i-drm.ko (sun4i/Makefile). A separate module cannot
    call it without a Makefile move and an export on top of the ops refactor.
  - What is mirrored is 60 lines, not 256. The rest of sun4i_crtc.c is vblank
    event handling for hardware whose vblank is wired, colour correction for
    the composite TV encoder, and an of_graph port lookup for a multi-pipe
    topology -- all absent here on purpose.

  So the refactor is the right long-term answer and belongs in the same patch
  as the mixer move, where it is one change instead of two.

THE VENDOR HAS A KMS DRIVER FOR THIS SoC, AND IT WAS READ FIRST
----------------------------------------------------------------

github.com/radxa/allwinner-bsp, branch cubie-aiot-v1.4.8, drivers/drm/ -- the
submodule `bsp` of radxa-pkg/linux-aw2511. In it, sunxi_drm_drv.c, sunxi_drm_crtc.c,
sunxi_device/hardware/lowlevel_de/sunxi_de.c and sunxi_device/sunxi_tcon.c
answer the same questions this patch does. Code is a HINT, not evidence, so:

CORROBORATED -- decided from the mainline side, and its running driver agrees:

  - the fb funcs above;
  - CRTC ENABLE BEFORE PLANE COMMIT. sunxi_drm_atomic_helper_commit_tail()
    (sunxi_drm_drv.c:186-203) hand-rolls exactly what
    drm_atomic_helper_commit_tail_rpm() gives: disables, modeset enables,
    commit_planes, drm_atomic_helper_fake_vblank();
  - THE SOURCE SWITCH BELONGS INSIDE THE TCON PROGRAM SEQUENCE.
    tcon_tv_src_select(..., LCD_SRC_DE, de_id) sits between the timing
    registers and tcon_tv_open() (sunxi_tcon.c:800-810), never after. `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source`
    writes TV_SRC_CTL at that same point; this patch changes the argument;
  - THE VBLANK INTERRUPT IS THE TCON'S (devm_request_irq on the TCON device,
    sunxi_tcon.c:472-484); the DE's own interrupt is CRC-only
    (sunxi_de.c:1125-1138). That is INTID 90 = GIC SPI 58 on our node.

TAUGHT -- three rules that are in no register table:

  1. THE BLENDER MUST BE PRODUCING AN OUTPUT SIZE BEFORE THE TCON IS OPENED.
     The vendor lifts the blender output attributes out of its deferred commit
     and writes them straight to MMIO because otherwise "tcon will think that
     de is enabled, but bld has no output, resulting in missing data"
     (sunxi_de.c:852-858). Here ORDER alone meets it -- `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` commits by direct
     store, so BLD_OUTSIZE has landed before the TCON is programmed -- and the
     CRTC enable says so, because enabling the TCON first would reproduce
     exactly that fault.
  2. A DATAPATH WRITE HAS A SAFE WINDOW INSIDE THE FRAME. Before arming its
     queue the vendor reads the TCON's current line and delays with an hrtimer
     until it lies in [vtotal/4, 3*vtotal/4] (rcq_update_timer_start(),
     sunxi_de.c:665-691); the TCON separately derives a per-mode deadline from
     its start delay and refuses updates past it (sunxi_tcon_calc_judge_line(),
     sunxi_tcon.c:121-150). Two independent guards on the write our page-flip
     path does unguarded. Named at sun60i_de_kms_scanout(); stage 3 owns it and
     needs the TCON interrupt first.
  3. THE PIXEL CLOCK IS THE MODE'S CLOCK DIVIDED BY PIXEL_MODE.
     clk_set_rate(mclk, pixel_clk / pixel_mode) with the same factor in
     TV_GCTL[5:4] (sunxi_tcon.c:801-806), the factor coming from the
     transmitter, which doubles it when a mode exceeds its own clock limit
     (edp_adjust_pixel_mode(), sunxi_drm_edp.c:708-721). Both sides of our pipe
     run one pixel per clock at their reset value, so the invariant holds today
     by neither half being touched. Recorded at sun60i_tcon_tv_kms_check(),
     because a mode list wider than one entry must carry the factor through
     three places at once.

DIFFERENT ON PURPOSE -- where the vendor driver differs at this exact layer,
and what this patch does instead. This patch does not merely reproduce the
vendor driver, so the differences are listed:

| The vendor | Ours |
|---|---|
| devm_drm_dev_alloc() inside a component master's .bind (drv.c:1232): the drm_device is released when the platform device goes away, not when the master unbinds, so a bind/unbind cycle leaks one | drm_dev_alloc() + drm_dev_put() in .unbind |
| drm_dev_register()'s return assigned and ignored (drv.c:1283); the function returns 0 either way, so a failed registration reports success | checked, and the bind unwinds |
| drm_mode_config_cleanup() in the error path (drv.c:1289) after drmm_mode_config_init() already registered that cleanup -- a double teardown | drmm only |
| the CRTC is configured from new_state->mode_blob, the mode USERSPACE asked for (crtc.c:1923-1926), so any adjustment an encoder makes in .atomic_check is discarded by the engine | both blocks take crtc_state->adjusted_mode |
| sunxi_de_enable()'s failure is logged and ignored -- scrtc->enabled = true and drm_crtc_vblank_on() run anyway (crtc.c:1955-1959) | the CRTC enable stops, and the engine is put back down if the TCON then fails |
| drm_crtc_vblank_get() in .atomic_begin (crtc.c:2035) with no drm_crtc_vblank_put() anywhere in that tree: after the first flip the vblank interrupt is pinned on and .disable_vblank is dead | no vblank claimed at all until it is serviced |
| no plane .atomic_check of any kind (crtc.c:1082-1092): no clipping, no scaling bound, no visibility -- and one of its two plane hooks needs an out-of-tree change to struct drm_plane_helper_funcs | the plane refuses what this datapath cannot do, before the commit |
| pixel_mode validated only at .atomic_enable, too late to refuse, and the validator accepts 0 and 8 (crtc.c:1945-1953) while the register writer handles 1, 2 and 4 -- 0 reaches pixel_clk / pixel_mode (sunxi_tcon.c:801), and only the eDP path ever sets the field | one mode, one pixel per clock, and the constraint named rather than half-checked |
| the engine is torn down with no quiesce and no delay between "output off" and clk_disable_unprepare() (sunxi_de.c:968-1010); that the TCON is already closed is guaranteed only by the DRM helper's ordering | sun60i_de_stop() disables the layer, waits a frame time, then drops the clocks |
| a hand-rolled connector with DRM_CONNECTOR_POLL_HPD (edp.c:4033) on a board whose HPD pad reads zero through a live link, so it is never polled and never notices a plug; and no drm_bridge anywhere, so drm_connector_oob_hotplug_event() from the Type-C alternate mode has no connector->fwnode to find | drm_bridge_connector_init(), which sets that fwnode and implements .oob_hotplug_event |
| about 1030 lines of its own fbdev (sunxi_fbdev_core.c, sunxi_fbdev_platform.c, sunxi_drmfb_core.c) beside an unused #include <drm/drm_fbdev_dma.h> | drm_client_setup() + drm_fbdev_dma |

And one thing it does better, because the list would be one-sided without it:
its CRTC survives a second display pipe, a writeback connector, gamma and a
bootloader handover. This one builds one pipe and refuses anything else by
name.

TWO OWNERS OF ONE DATAPATH, AND HOW THAT IS PREVENTED
------------------------------------------------------

`drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` and `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` are driven by debugfs verbs that program the very registers a
modeset programs, and this tree's diagnostics rule says those verbs are never
deleted. So the WRITE verbs are refused with -EBUSY while a KMS master holds
the component, the READ verbs (`state`, `scanout`) are not, and the A/B arm
survives two ways with no different kernel: boot a device tree without the
display-engine node, or unbind the sun60i-a733-kms platform device by hand.
The handshake is the component bind, which also refuses to take a device that
debugfs has already enabled.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, named so it is not read as an oversight
---------------------------------------------------------------------

  - VBLANK. drm_vblank_init() is not called and the CRTC has no
    .enable_vblank. TCON_TV1's interrupt is in the device tree since `arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window` and
    nothing requests it; `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` writes TV_GINT0 to zero on the stated grounds
    that "an enabled interrupt with no handler is not a diagnostic, it is a
    latch nobody clears". Claiming a vblank that never counts would be worse
    than having none: drm_atomic_helper_wait_for_vblanks() would wait on a
    stopped counter. Without drm_vblank_init(), drm_dev_has_vblank() is false,
    every crtc_state->no_vblank is set by drm_atomic_helper_check_modeset(),
    and drm_atomic_helper_fake_vblank() completes each flip event immediately.
    Flips therefore return, with no guarantee about where in the frame they
    landed. That is stage 3, and it is why the tearing test lives there.
  - MORE THAN ONE PLANE, MORE THAN ONE FORMAT, ANY SCALING. XRGB8888, linear,
    full-screen at the origin, because that is what `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` has on silicon. The
    VSU8 scaler is in the channel datapath unconditionally on this DE
    generation and `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` stages it at unity; a scaled or offset plane needs real
    coefficients, which is stage 4.
  - A DEVICE-TREE BINDING. No display node in this tree has one -- not the
    mixer, not the TCON, not the transmitter -- and adding a schema for the
    pipeline node alone while the blocks it points at have none would describe
    the least of it. The debt is real and it is the whole display track's.
  - AN IOMMU. Checked rather than assumed: the framebuffer is allocated against
    the DRM device and fetched by the display engine, two different devices,
    and the addresses are interchangeable only while neither is translated.
    sun60i_kms_check_dma_domain() compares them once at bind and refuses rather
    than producing a black screen whose every register reads back correct.

CORRECTED 2026-09-22, comments only: SUN60I_KMS_DMA_BITS and the probe
comment gave the 32-bit mask as a register width. The DE's layers have a
high-address byte (VI +0x0d0..+0x0e4);
`drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` does not program it, so the 32-bit limit is a driver
choice. The plane check's message still names a 32-bit register.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig
@@ -63,3 +63,32 @@ config DRM_SUN60I_DE
 	  reset. It is not a KMS driver and registers no CRTC yet.
 
 	  Say M here if you want this SoC to be able to scan a framebuffer.
+
+config DRM_SUN60I_KMS
+	tristate "Allwinner A733 (sun60iw2) display pipe (KMS)"
+	depends on DRM && OF
+	depends on ARCH_SUNXI || COMPILE_TEST
+	depends on DRM_SUN60I_DE && DRM_SUN60I_TCON_TV
+	select DRM_BRIDGE_CONNECTOR
+	select DRM_CLIENT_SELECTION
+	select DRM_DISPLAY_HELPER
+	select DRM_GEM_DMA_HELPER
+	select DRM_KMS_HELPER
+	help
+	  Enable this for the KMS driver that ties the A733 display path
+	  together: a drm_device, one CRTC on TCON_TV1, one plane out of one
+	  display-engine channel, and the connector the DisplayPort bridge
+	  hangs on. It is what creates /dev/fb0 and, with
+	  FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE, puts a console on the screen.
+
+	  It drives no register itself. Every write goes through the display
+	  engine and TCON drivers it depends on, which is why both are hard
+	  dependencies rather than selects: each is independently useful
+	  without this one, and each has its own debugfs verbs that this
+	  driver takes ownership of while it is bound.
+
+	  The DisplayPort transmitter is NOT a dependency. It publishes a
+	  drm_bridge that this driver finds through the device-tree graph, so
+	  a kernel without it simply has no output on this pipe.
+
+	  Say M here if you want this SoC to be able to run a KMS console.
